// Practice quiz: Project setup with npm and Vite
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/dom-tooling-and-async/project-setup-with-npm-and-vite

::o1 double dash::In npm create vite@latest my-app -- --template vanilla, what does the bare double dash do? {
=It passes the options that follow through npm to the scaffolder #Correct. Without it, npm may treat them as its own options.
~It comments out the rest of the line #Not quite. Shell comments use a different character.
~It runs the command twice #Not quite. It only separates arguments.
~It selects the latest version #Not quite. The version comes from the at-latest part.
}

::o2 committing::Which pair belongs in version control for a Node.js project? {
=package.json and package-lock.json #Correct. Together they let anyone reproduce the install.
~package.json and node_modules #Not quite. node_modules is reproducible and is not committed.
~package-lock.json and node_modules #Not quite. package.json is needed, and node_modules is not committed.
~Only node_modules #Not quite. That is the one that stays out.
}

::o2 ranges::package.json says a dependency range of ^7.0.0. What pins the exact version installed? {
=package-lock.json #Correct. The lockfile records what the range resolved to.
~The caret character itself #Not quite. A caret allows a range of compatible versions.
~.gitignore #Not quite. It only lists files for Git to ignore.
~The node_modules folder name #Not quite. It holds packages but is not the source of truth.
}

::o3 test tool group::Your project runs vitest only in CI and locally. Which command adds it? {
=npm install --save-dev vitest #Correct. Test tools belong in devDependencies.
~npm install vitest --production #Not quite. That is not how to mark a development tool.
~npm ci vitest #Not quite. npm ci installs from the lockfile and takes no package name.
~npm run vitest #Not quite. npm run only runs existing scripts.
}

::o3 lockfile missing::npm ci stops with an error saying it needs an existing package-lock.json. What should you do? {
=Run npm install, then review and commit the generated lockfile #Correct. That creates the lockfile for future exact installs.
~Delete package.json and try again #Not quite. Then npm has nothing to read.
~Add node_modules to the repository #Not quite. That does not supply a lockfile.
~Use npm run dev instead #Not quite. That does not install anything.
}
